{
"dur": 99.187,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 7.44,
"text": "By 2006, America was in a housing boom. Lenders were handing out mortgages with little or no proof that borrowers could pay.",
"end": 7.715
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 7.715,
"vo_start": 7.99,
"vo_end": 12.732,
"text": "The film The Big Short, based on Michael Lewis's book, caught the mood in one scene.",
"end": 13.007
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 13.007,
"vo_start": 13.282,
"vo_end": 20.114,
"text": "A money manager meets a dancer at a Florida club. She tells him she owns five houses and a condo, all bought with loans.",
"end": 20.389
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 20.389,
"vo_start": 20.664,
"vo_end": 27.382,
"text": "She is a film character, but loans like hers were real. Many borrowers could only keep paying if house prices kept going up.",
"end": 27.657
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 27.657,
"vo_start": 27.932,
"vo_end": 37.369,
"text": "Inside JPMorgan, the warning came from a monthly report. In October 2006, Dimon heard that late payments on subprime loans were rising fast across the industry.",
"end": 37.644
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 37.644,
"vo_start": 37.919,
"vo_end": 44.998,
"text": "According to Fortune, he phoned the head of the bank's securitized products business, who was on holiday in Africa, and told him to start selling.",
"end": 45.273
},
{
"i": 7,
"start": 45.273,
"vo_start": 45.548,
"vo_end": 51.388,
"text": "JPMorgan cut back hard on subprime, including selling more than twelve billion dollars of subprime mortgages it had made.",
"end": 51.663
},
{
"i": 8,
"start": 51.663,
"vo_start": 51.938,
"vo_end": 59.008,
"text": "Now go back to our two banks, and give them a bad year. Five percent of their loans go bad. That is a five dollar loss each.",
"end": 59.283
},
{
"i": 9,
"start": 59.283,
"vo_start": 59.558,
"vo_end": 66.358,
"text": "Bank A takes the hit out of its eight dollars of capital and still has three left. It survives.",
"end": 66.633
},
{
"i": 10,
"start": 66.633,
"vo_start": 66.908,
"vo_end": 75.069,
"text": "Bank B only had three. After a five dollar loss, it is two dollars below zero. It cannot pay what it owes, so it fails.",
"end": 75.344
},
{
"i": 11,
"start": 75.344,
"vo_start": 75.619,
"vo_end": 85.739,
"text": "That is 2008 in real numbers. At the end of 2007, Bear Stearns had about three hundred and ninety-five billion dollars of assets on less than twelve billion of its own capital.",
"end": 86.014
},
{
"i": 12,
"start": 86.014,
"vo_start": 86.289,
"vo_end": 90.569,
"text": "That is roughly thirty-three to one. Lehman Brothers was around thirty to one.",
"end": 90.844
},
{
"i": 13,
"start": 90.844,
"vo_start": 91.119,
"vo_end": 98.237,
"text": "JPMorgan had about one point five six trillion dollars of assets and around a hundred and twenty-three billion of equity. About thirteen to one.",
"end": 99.187
}
],
